{"name":"Birchwood","hex":"#ccbeac","fetched":"2026-10-11","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"Birchwood","whole":true,"parts":[{"part":"Proper noun","definitions":["A civil parish in Warrington borough, Cheshire, England .","A housing estate of Lincoln borough, Lincolnshire, England ."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/Birchwood"}],"entries":[{"title":"Birch wood","blocks":[{"text":"Birch wood is a type of wood of the birch. Birch wood is pale yellow-brown wood having a close, straight grain and uniform texture that finishes to a smooth surface. Sometimes it is dyed to imitate mahogany. This type of wood is used for among others firewood, turnery, furniture, cabinetry, tool handles, hoops, plywood, flooring and shoe heels."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/e/e0/Betula_pendula_wood_ray_section_1_beentree.jpg/330px-Betula_pendula_wood_ray_section_1_beentree.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Birch_wood"},{"title":"Birch","blocks":[{"text":"A birch is a thin-leaved deciduous hardwood tree of the genus Betula, in the family Betulaceae, which also includes alders, hazels, and hornbeams. It is closely related to the beech-oak family Fagaceae. The genus Betula contains 30 to 60 known taxa of which 11 are on the IUCN 2011 Red List of Threatened Species. They are typically short-lived pioneer species and are widespread in the Northern Hemisphere, particularly in northern areas of temperate climates and in boreal climates. Birch wood is used for a wide range of purposes."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/4/46/Betula_pendula_001.jpg/330px-Betula_pendula_001.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Birch"}],"images":[{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/8/81/Birke_Multiplex.JPG/500px-Birke_Multiplex.JPG?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=p